      "holds": {
        "dq-g-gv1": "The key treats the portal route as a third independent circulation and makes an internal elastic lamina a universal component of the intima. The hepatic portal route is part of systemic circulation, and a distinct lamina is characteristic of arteries rather than all vessels. Check the intended course convention.",
        "dq-g-gv5": "The kidney is the intended answer, with fenestrated glomerular endothelium and a continuous basal lamina, but adrenal medullary sinusoids are also described as fenestrated with a continuous basal lamina. Specify which histological definition of discontinuous is intended, or replace the adrenal option.",
        "dq-g-gv6": "The vein-wall statement keyed true excludes an elastic lamina altogether, but histological descriptions recognise a thin or inconspicuous internal elastic lamina in some veins. Qualify the claim before explaining it as a rule.",
        "dq-g-gvs9": "The right pulmonary artery is longer and larger, but the keyed answer adds that it passes under the aortic arch. The defining right-sided route is behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, while the left pulmonary artery emerges within the concavity of the arch. Clarify the intended relation and revise the option if needed.",
        "dq-g-gvg11": "The left pulmonary artery is anterior to the descending aorta, but its relation to the left main bronchus changes along its course. At the root it arches superior to the left main bronchus before descending posterolaterally. Specify the segment if retaining the keyed anterior relation.",
        "dq-g-gvh1": "The key names the hepatic portal pathway as a third circulation alongside systemic and pulmonary. Standard anatomy treats it as a specialised route within the systemic circulation. Confirm the course-specific classification and state it in the stem if intended.",
        "dq-g-gvh3": "The elastic lamina is prominent in many arterial intimas, but is inconspicuous or absent in many veins, and capillaries have no equivalent multilayered intima. Specify an artery or the vessel type intended by the keyed description.",
        "dq-g-gvh4": "The key equates an arteriovenous shunt with a metarteriole. In the capillary-bed model a metarteriole can feed the throughfare channel, while direct arteriovenous anastomoses are a distinct arrangement. Check the lecture terminology and clarify which structure is intended.",
        "dq-g-gvh8": "The keyed ulnar artery is clinically palpable at the wrist, and the stem does not identify a specific list. If it tests the lecture's chosen list, cite that list or replace the distractor with a genuinely nonpalpable artery.",
        "dq-g-gvh19": "The median sacral artery arises from the posterior aspect of the aorta near the bifurcation. The coeliac, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries are the three unpaired anterior visceral branches, so there is no correct fourth ventral branch among the options.",
        "dq-g-go3": "The stem includes blood and lymphatic vessels, but the key applies the mucosa–muscularis–adventitia model and immediate luminal epithelium across hollow organs. Vascular channels have endothelium and their own tunics, while many digestive segments have a distinct submucosa. Specify the system before keying the general wall plan."
      },
